{#-
  The shell every page of the site is built into. A page supplies its own <main> and nothing else:
  the doctype, the head, the stylesheet links, the logo and the constellation it opens, the persona
  floating opposite it, the feed of every world and the persona sheet are written once, here.

  This is not the sort of website that uses conventional navigation (issue #54). There is no app
  bar: two things float over the page and they are the whole of the navigation -- the sparkles logo
  in the upper left, which says the site's name on rollover and opens a constellation of options on
  tap, and the persona in the upper right, itself a small constellation.

  The visual language is Material Design 3 (m3.material.io): one tonal colour scheme derived from a
  few seed colours (_sass/_tokens.scss), the M3 type scale, the M3 shape scale, state layers on
  everything that can be pressed, and tonal surfaces instead of borders. The components the shell
  is made of are the M3 ones -- a floating button and the tonal chips it opens, a basic dialog for
  the sheet, and cards in a masonry feed -- so the whole site reads as one system.

  Front matter a page may set:
    title       the page's name, the same words every link to it uses (see _data/worlds.json):
                every page sets it, index.html included ("the threshold"), and " · interesting"
                is appended to it in the <title>.
    description the <meta name='description'>: one plain sentence a search result or a shared
                link shows, omitted when absent.
    stylesheet  a name in css/: "quiet-room" links css/quiet-room.css after the shared
                css/site.css, so the page's own rules win over the shared ones. Omit it for a page
                that needs none.
    inlineStyles
                write the styles into the page instead of linking them. Only error.html needs it:
                CloudFront returns that page for any 404, at whatever path was asked for, so a
                relative <link> next to it would be a guess.
    siteRoot    the site's root as a path, for the same page and the same reason: with it set
                to "/", every shared script and link is written from the root, so a visitor who
                lands on the 404 page at /some/deep/path still gets the scripts and can leave.
                It is the one place the site names where it is served from: "/" is right for
                makeitmoreinteresting.com, and a copy served under a sub-path sets that sub-path
                here instead ("/interesting/"). Every other page leaves it out and keeps
                relative links, so they work under any sub-path unchanged.

  What a page is: its <main> is the feature. It is unbordered, it fills the first screen (the
  viewport less the margin that lets the feed peek over the fold -- nothing sits above it any
  more, and it keeps the room the two floating marks need clear at the top), its background is the
  page's own palette bleeding to the viewport's edges, and its content sits in a centred column
  (every direct child of <main> lands in that column; see _sass/_panel.scss).
  A page sets nothing about navigation: the logo, the persona and the feed are the whole of the
  shared chrome, and they are the same on every page.

  The shared chrome, and why it is this size:
    the logo     a sparkles mark in the upper left, floating over the page, and nothing else at
                 rest. Rolling over it (or focusing it) fades the site's name in beside it,
                 quickly and tightly; pressing it turns the page into a lightbox -- dimmed,
                 blurred, its animation held and its controls inert -- and branches the options
                 out from the logo as a constellation (_sass/_nav.scss for the geometry,
                 js/site.js for the lightbox and the options that come and go). It is a <details>
                 element, so with scripting switched off it is still a disclosure a visitor can
                 open, and its options are plain links either way. Pressing it never navigates:
                 the threshold is the home icon in the near orbit.
                 The near orbit is where the site's destinations are -- the threshold, the mood
                 atlas, site map, and the world a reading opens onto once there is one -- and the
                 far orbit is the apparatus: "cookies", "state", privacy, terms.
    the persona  the one thing a visitor configures here, shown the way an app shows its account:
                 a round portrait of their sky floating in the upper right, which opens the sheet
                 (js/persona.js). With no persona yet the portrait ring is dashed and the button
                 reads "set up persona", filled and beckoning, so the first thing to do is the
                 first thing seen; once a reading exists a "go to" link to the world it opens
                 onto sits beside it. The one sentence on where things stand is written for
                 screen readers and never takes space. On the threshold the sideways question is
                 asked in the page's own feature (index.html hosts #persona-probe), never in the
                 chrome.
    the feed     every world as a card, in a masonry grid that keeps going as a visitor scrolls
                 (_includes/worlds.njk, from _data/worlds.json, and js/feed.js with one module per
                 world in js/modules/). It has no caption: the grid speaks for itself. The cards
                 the template writes are plain links, so index.html's own markup links every
                 world and the reachability axiom holds with scripting off; the script lays them
                 out as columns, paints each one, and deals more between them without end.
                 sitemap.html and moods.html list every page themselves, so they are the two
                 pages without it.
    the sheet    the persona's dialog, written after everything else and shown only when opened:
                 the constellation (stars placed, dragged and read) and the orientation (the
                 question, and what it read). Its headings come after the page's own, so the
                 heading outline a screen reader walks stays in order.

  Seven shared lines in the <head>:
    js/analytics.js  the Google Analytics tag and the consent banner that gates it (see
                     ANALYTICS_TAG and check_analytics in .github/scripts/make_interesting.py).
    js/state.js      the one local-state document every page reads and writes through, and the
                     meta menu that exports it (STATE_TAG and check_state).
    js/participate.js
                     the one way a visitor can steer this site: the prominent button in the middle
                     of the bottom edge that opens a pre-shaped new issue (PARTICIPATE_TAG and
                     check_participate).
    js/persona.js    the persona: the sky several worlds read, kept and changed in one place, plus
                     the avatar and the sheet above. Not deferred, so a page's own script can read
                     window.interestingPersona.stars() while the body is parsed.
    js/site.js       the shared helpers a page can call: window.interestingSite.unlock(), which
                     renders a part that needs a saved sky as powered down with the one button
                     that powers it (see the README, "Powered down, never broken"), plus the main
                     nav -- the logo's lightbox, the options that come and go with the state, and
                     the two corner affordances it adopts -- the slider fill and each stage's
                     aspect ratio. Not deferred either, both because a page's own script may call
                     it and because it marks the document as scripted before the body is drawn.
    js/threshold.js  the mood flow's engine: the orientations, the library of query mechanisms,
                     the clock and time-zone signals and the partly-remembered reading. The
                     threshold's feature and the persona sheet are where it is seen (MOOD_TAG and
                     check_mood).
    js/feed.js       the feed: lays the world cards out as columns, paints them through the
                     modules in js/modules/, keeps dealing cards as the visitor scrolls, and
                     paints the threshold's feature (window.interestingFeed.feature). An ES
                     module, so it loads each world's module only when a card of it is near.
  Four of them may never be dropped: a built page missing the analytics, state, participation or
  mood line is refused. The files behind the first three are fixed; js/threshold.js is protected
  instead, so a run may rewrite the flow but never delete it. The other three are ordinary site
  source.

  Three corner affordances those fixed files draw are a cadre of their own, outside this site's
  visual language and outside what any page may restyle or reword. "steer the site" keeps the
  bottom edge, and nothing of the site's goes there. The other two -- "cookies" and "state" -- are
  adopted by the shell into the far orbit of the constellation, where a visitor finds them with
  everything else: js/site.js hides the two buttons their own files pin to the corners and forwards
  the constellation's options to them, so there is still exactly one cookies dialog and one state
  menu, and neither fixed file is edited to do it.

  The page's own world, when it has one, is named on <html data-world> so _sass/_mood.scss gives
  the page that world's palette; a visitor's reading lands on <html data-mood> and wins over it.

  Links are relative, like every link in the site: it has no fixed domain and may be served under
  a sub-path, so "css/site.css" and not "/css/site.css".
-#}
{%- set here = (page.filePathStem | replace('/', '')) + '.html' if page else '' %}
{%- set thisWorld = null %}
{%- for world in worlds.worlds %}{% if world.file == here %}{% set thisWorld = world %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
{%- set icons = {
  'home': 'M10 20v-6h4v6h5v-8h3L12 3 2 12h3v8z',
  'explore': 'M12 10.9c-.61 0-1.1.49-1.1 1.1s.49 1.1 1.1 1.1c.61 0 1.1-.49 1.1-1.1s-.49-1.1-1.1-1.1zM12 2C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10S17.52 2 12 2zm2.19 12.19L6 18l3.81-8.19L18 6l-3.81 8.19z',
  'map': 'M20.5 3l-.16.03L15 5.1 9 3 3.36 4.9c-.21.07-.36.25-.36.48V20.5c0 .28.22.5.5.5l.16-.03L9 18.9l6 2.1 5.64-1.9c.21-.07.36-.25.36-.48V3.5c0-.28-.22-.5-.5-.5zM15 19l-6-2.11V5l6 2.11V19z',
  'spark': 'M19 9l1.25-2.75L23 5l-2.75-1.25L19 1l-1.25 2.75L15 5l2.75 1.25L19 9zm-7.5.5L9 4 6.5 9.5 1 12l5.5 2.5L9 20l2.5-5.5L17 12l-5.5-2.5zM19 15l-1.25 2.75L15 19l2.75 1.25L19 23l1.25-2.75L23 19l-2.75-1.25L19 15z',
  'star': 'M12 17.27L18.18 21l-1.64-7.03L22 9.24l-7.19-.61L12 2 9.19 8.63 2 9.24l5.46 4.73L5.82 21z',
  'shield': 'M12 1L3 5v6c0 5.55 3.84 10.74 9 12 5.16-1.26 9-6.45 9-12V5l-9-4z',
  'page': 'M14 2H6c-1.1 0-1.99.9-1.99 2L4 20c0 1.1.89 2 1.99 2H18c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2V8l-6-6zm2 16H8v-2h8v2zm0-4H8v-2h8v2zm-3-5V3.5L18.5 9H13z',
  'tune': 'M3 17v2h6v-2H3zM3 5v2h10V5H3zm10 16v-2h8v-2h-8v-2h-2v6h2zM7 9v2H3v2h4v2h2V9H7zm14 4v-2H11v2h10zm-6-4h2V7h4V5h-4V3h-2v6z',
  'swap': 'M16 17.01V10h-2v7.01h-3L15 21l4-3.99h-3zM9 3L5 6.99h3V14h2V6.99h3L9 3z'
} %}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang='en' data-page='{{ here }}'{% if thisWorld %} data-world='{{ thisWorld.mood }}'{% endif %}{% if siteRoot %} data-root='{{ siteRoot }}'{% endif %}>
<head>
  <meta charset='utf-8'>
  <meta name='viewport' content='width=device-width, initial-scale=1'>
  <meta name='color-scheme' content='dark'>
  <title>{% if title %}{{ title }} · {% endif %}interesting</title>
  <link rel='icon' href='{{ siteRoot }}favicon.svg' type='image/svg+xml'>
{%- if description %}
  <meta name='description' content='{{ description }}'>
{%- endif %}
{%- if inlineStyles %}
  <style>{{ 'site' | css | safe }}{{ stylesheet | css | safe }}</style>
{%- else %}
  <link rel='stylesheet' href='css/site.css'>
{%- if stylesheet %}
  <link rel='stylesheet' href='css/{{ stylesheet }}.css'>
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if siteRoot %}
  {#- The 404 page is served at any missing path, so its scripts are named from the site's root;
      see the note on siteRoot above. The seven lines are the same seven as below. -#}
  <script src='{{ siteRoot }}js/analytics.js' defer></script>
  <script src='{{ siteRoot }}js/state.js'></script>
  <script src='{{ siteRoot }}js/participate.js' defer></script>
  <script src='{{ siteRoot }}js/persona.js'></script>
  <script src='{{ siteRoot }}js/site.js'></script>
  <script src='{{ siteRoot }}js/threshold.js' defer></script>
  <script src='{{ siteRoot }}js/feed.js' type='module'></script>
{%- else %}
  <!-- Analytics and the cookie consent banner that gates it; see js/analytics.js. -->
  <script src='js/analytics.js' defer></script>
  <!-- The one local-state store every page reads and writes through, and its meta menu; see
       js/state.js. Not deferred: a page's own script runs before any deferred one does. -->
  <script src='js/state.js'></script>
  <!-- The one way a visitor can steer this site: the button in the middle of the bottom edge that
       opens a pre-shaped new issue; see js/participate.js. -->
  <script src='js/participate.js' defer></script>
  <!-- The persona: the sky several worlds read, and the avatar and sheet it is set up in; see
       js/persona.js. Not deferred either, for the same reason, and after the store it writes
       through. -->
  <script src='js/persona.js'></script>
  <!-- The shared helpers; see js/site.js. After the persona, whose sky its unlock helper reads. -->
  <script src='js/site.js'></script>
  <!-- The mood flow's engine: ask before offering; see js/threshold.js. Deferred, and so after
       js/state.js, which is what it keeps its reading in. -->
  <script src='js/threshold.js' defer></script>
  <!-- The feed; see js/feed.js. A module script runs deferred too, after the flow it reads. -->
  <script src='js/feed.js' type='module'></script>
{%- endif %}
</head>
<body>
  <a class='skip-link' href='#main-content'>skip to main content</a>
  {#- The main nav: the logo, and the constellation it opens. A <details>, so the disclosure is
      the browser's own -- operable by keyboard, and still an open-and-close affordance with
      scripting switched off, where the options lay out as a cascade under the logo instead of a
      constellation around it. js/site.js adds the lightbox, the stagger, and the options that
      come and go with the state; _sass/_nav.scss places them. Every option is written here, so
      index.html's own markup leads to every page of the site and the reachability axiom holds
      with no script at all. -#}
  <details class='sparknav' id='sparknav'>
    <summary class='sparknav-logo' id='sparknav-logo' aria-label='interesting: the site menu'>
      <svg class='icon sparknav-spark' viewBox='0 0 24 24' aria-hidden='true' focusable='false'><path d='{{ icons.spark }}'/></svg>
      <span class='sparknav-name'>interesting</span>
    </summary>
    {#- The lightbox: it dims and blurs everything behind it, and takes the press that closes the
        constellation again. js/site.js holds the page's motion and makes it inert to match. -#}
    <div class='sparknav-veil' id='sparknav-veil'></div>
    <nav class='sparknav-sky' aria-label='Site'>
      <ul class='sparknav-orbit sparknav-near' id='sparknav-near'>
{%- for link in worlds.wayIn %}
        <li class='sparknav-option'>
          <span class='sparknav-ray' aria-hidden='true'></span>
          <a class='sparknav-node' href='{{ siteRoot }}{{ link.file }}'{% if here == link.file %} aria-current='page'{% endif %}>
            <svg class='icon' viewBox='0 0 24 24' aria-hidden='true' focusable='false'><path d='{{ icons[link.icon] }}'/></svg>
            <span class='sparknav-label'>{{ link.name }}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
{%- endfor %}
        {#- The world a reading opens onto: in the orbit only once something has been read, and
            named for that world (js/site.js, from window.threshold). -#}
        <li class='sparknav-option' id='sparknav-reading' hidden>
          <span class='sparknav-ray' aria-hidden='true'></span>
          <a class='sparknav-node' id='sparknav-reading-go' href='{{ siteRoot }}moods.html'>
            <svg class='icon' viewBox='0 0 24 24' aria-hidden='true' focusable='false'><path d='{{ icons.star }}'/></svg>
            <span class='sparknav-label' id='sparknav-reading-label'>your world</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <ul class='sparknav-orbit sparknav-far' id='sparknav-far'>
        {#- The two adopted affordances. Each is in the orbit only while the file that owns it has
            drawn its own button, which js/site.js then hides and presses on this one's behalf --
            js/analytics.js and js/state.js are fixed files and are never edited for this. -#}
        <li class='sparknav-option' id='sparknav-cookies' hidden>
          <span class='sparknav-ray' aria-hidden='true'></span>
          <button type='button' class='sparknav-node' id='sparknav-cookies-open' aria-haspopup='dialog'>
            <svg class='icon' viewBox='0 0 24 24' aria-hidden='true' focusable='false'><path d='{{ icons.tune }}'/></svg>
            <span class='sparknav-label'>cookies</span>
          </button>
        </li>
        <li class='sparknav-option' id='sparknav-state' hidden>
          <span class='sparknav-ray' aria-hidden='true'></span>
          <button type='button' class='sparknav-node' id='sparknav-state-open' aria-haspopup='dialog'>
            <svg class='icon' viewBox='0 0 24 24' aria-hidden='true' focusable='false'><path d='{{ icons.swap }}'/></svg>
            <span class='sparknav-label' id='sparknav-state-label'>state</span>
          </button>
        </li>
{%- for link in worlds.finePrint %}
        <li class='sparknav-option'>
          <span class='sparknav-ray' aria-hidden='true'></span>
          <a class='sparknav-node' href='{{ siteRoot }}{{ link.file }}'{% if here == link.file %} aria-current='page'{% endif %}>
            <svg class='icon' viewBox='0 0 24 24' aria-hidden='true' focusable='false'><path d='{{ icons[link.icon] }}'/></svg>
            <span class='sparknav-label'>{{ link.name }}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
{%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </details>
  {#- The persona, floating in the upper right: the only other navigation on the site. What a
      visitor with scripting switched off gets is written here and claims nothing; js/persona.js
      rewrites the sentence, draws the portrait and un-hides the two controls. -#}
  <div class='persona' id='persona' data-state='empty' data-sky='none' data-reading='none' data-asking='false'>
    <p class='persona-text visually-hidden' id='persona-text'>No persona yet.</p>
    <div class='persona-actions'>
      <a class='action persona-go' id='persona-go' href='{{ siteRoot }}moods.html' hidden>go to your world</a>
      <button type='button' class='persona-avatar' id='persona-open' aria-haspopup='dialog' aria-controls='persona-sheet' aria-describedby='persona-text' hidden>
        <span class='persona-portrait' aria-hidden='true'><canvas id='persona-portrait' width='40' height='40'></canvas></span>
        <span class='persona-label'>set up persona</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id='main-content' tabindex='-1'>{{ content | safe }}</div>
{%- if not (here == 'sitemap.html' or here == 'moods.html') %}
  <div class='page'>
{% include 'worlds.njk' %}
  </div>
{%- endif %}
  {#- The persona sheet: closed until the avatar opens it, and floating over the page when it does.
      Written last so its headings follow the page's own. -#}
  <dialog class='persona-sheet' id='persona-sheet' aria-labelledby='persona-sheet-title'>
    <div class='persona-sheet-head'>
      <h2 id='persona-sheet-title' class='persona-sheet-title'>your persona</h2>
      <button type='button' class='persona-close btn-text' id='persona-close'>close</button>
    </div>
    <p class='panel-note persona-sheet-intro'>A small sky of stars you place, and what the site has read of you. Every world reads from both, and both stay in this browser.</p>
    <section class='persona-section' aria-labelledby='persona-sky-title'>
      <h3 id='persona-sky-title' class='panel-label'>constellation</h3>
      <p class='panel-note'>Tap the sky to place a star, drag a star to move it, tap one to read its thought. Several worlds read this one sky, each its own way.</p>
      <div class='persona-sky' id='persona-sky'><canvas class='persona-sky-canvas' aria-hidden='true'></canvas></div>
      <div class='controls'>
        <button id='persona-drop' type='button'>drop a star</button>
        <button id='persona-seed' type='button'>seed a small sky</button>
        <button id='persona-remove' type='button' hidden>remove this star</button>
        <button id='persona-clear' type='button' class='btn-text'>clear the sky</button>
      </div>
      <p class='panel-status' id='persona-sky-status' aria-live='polite'></p>
    </section>
    <section class='persona-section' aria-labelledby='persona-reading-title'>
      <h3 id='persona-reading-title' class='panel-label'>orientation</h3>
      <p class='panel-status' id='persona-reading'></p>
      <div class='controls persona-reading-actions'>
        <button id='persona-ask' type='button'>ask me</button>
        <button id='persona-forget' type='button' class='btn-text' hidden>forget my reading</button>
        <a class='action' id='persona-reading-go' href='{{ siteRoot }}moods.html' hidden>go to that world</a>
      </div>
      <div class='persona-probe' id='persona-sheet-probe' hidden></div>
      <p class='panel-note'>It never asks you to describe yourself, and it never asks the same way twice. <a href='{{ siteRoot }}moods.html'>The mood atlas</a> shows every way it asks and every answer it can arrive at.</p>
    </section>
  </dialog>
</body>
</html>
